{"id": "a35ff4f3-e574-47a6-a2ff-fd8d5ed4542e", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:24:28.013214", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "adda98dc-2ee8-4032-88f0-ea7ad01597f2", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-7ae08341-5d6e-4cdd-9c01-18ba47455026", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"ls -la /home/actor 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; ls -la /tmp 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; ls -la /workspace/.* 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo '---'; find / -maxdepth 4 -iname '*.md' 2>/dev/null | grep -iE 'opro|bbh|big.bench|track' | head -20; echo '---'; find / -maxdepth 4 -iname 'track.md' 2>/dev/null\", \"exit_code\": 1, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.064, \"output\": \"total 0\\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 1776737281 748400513 40 Oct 23 01:24 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 1776737281 748400513 60 Oct 23 01:24 ..\\n---\\ntotal 0\\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 1776737281 748400513  40 Oct 23 01:24 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 1776737281 748400513 240 Oct 23 01:24 ..\\n---\\n---\\n---\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "adda98dc-2ee8-4032-88f0-ea7ad01597f2", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 11}
{"id": "5b63f357-863b-45cf-91cb-6d610a7010fc", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:25:35.104023", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "a35ff4f3-e574-47a6-a2ff-fd8d5ed4542e", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-a96850f5-da6a-4b23-997e-9f7fec93ece5", "tool_call": {"id": "call-a96850f5-da6a-4b23-997e-9f7fec93ece5", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Hand off first-turn plan\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## 1) Inspection of /workspace\\n\\nThe workspace is effectively empty:\\n\\n```\\n/workspace/\\n└── task.json      (only file; 445 bytes)\\n```\\n\\nContents of `task.json`:\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"question\\\": \\\"Do the optimized instructions discovered by OPRO outperform the baseline instructions ('Let's think step by step.' and the empty prompt) on BBH tasks?\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question...\\\",\\n  \\\"documents\\\": { \\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNotable observations:\\n- **No `track.md` is actually present** anywhere on disk; the `documents` field is a dead reference. The adjacent note in `task.json` itself (\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\") confirms this is a pure design deliverable.\\n- **No code, no datasets, no scratch dirs.** Recursive search for `*opro*`, `*bbh*`, `*.md`, `track.md` returns no hits beyond `/workspace/task.json`.\\n- **Environment constraints from the task envelope:** bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, no GPU, 24 CPU cores, system Python only with no preinstalled ML/data deps. No local model server available, no HuggingFace cache, no BBH JSONL files.\\n- **Implication:** this design cannot piggy-back on locally installed tooling. Anything resembling an executable experiment would have to either (i) shell out to an external API (network is disabled, so not feasible here) or (ii) reproduce state from scratch. Both are out of scope for \\\"design-only.\\\" Therefore the answer below is a fully specified design without claiming any execution.\\n\\n## 2) Proposed experimental design\\n\\n### (a) Optimized instructions under test\\n\\n| Condition | Instruction text | Source / role |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **I1** (empty baseline) | *(none, prompt = task question only)* | Asked in the question |\\n| **I2** (CoT baseline) | `Let's think step by step.` | Kojima et al. 2022; asked in the question |\\n| **I3** (OPRO leader) | `Take a deep breath` | Reported top-scoring OPRO-discovered instruction on BBH with PaLM 2-L (Yang et al., \\\"Large Language Models as Optimizers,\\\" ICLR 2024) |\\n| **I4** (OPRO leader) | `Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer` | Cited in your prompt; treat as a second OPRO-discovered instruction (provenance flagged below) |\\n| **I5** (sanity control, optional) | Original per-subtask BBH instruction preamble | Ensures the comparison isolates the *instruction-tuning* effect of I3/I4, not chain-of-thought in general |\\n\\nUsing **two discovered leaders** plus **two baselines** (and an optional sanity condition) matches your question and gives a small, factorial design that supports paired comparisons.\\n\\n### (b) Task sampling across BBH subtasks\\n\\nBBH (\\\"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them,\\\" Suzgun et al. 2022) has **23 subtasks**, ~6,500 examples in the canonical `test_suite` split, organized into 7 categories (algebra, formal logic, natural language, knowledge, multistep arithmetic, common sense, programming).\\n\\nDesign choices:\\n\\n1. **Full suite, capped.** Use **all 23 subtasks**, sampling up to **250 examples per subtask** from the official test split → **≤ 5,750 prompts per condition**. Full subtask coverage is essential because OPRO's leaderboard is reported as the macro-average over those 23 subtasks; restricting to a subset changes the published comparison metric.\\n2. **Split discipline (critical for validity).** OPRO's instruction optimization used the BBH **train + validation** portion (the 5,500-example \\\"training\\\" subset the authors released for prompt-tuning experiments). The evaluation here MUST use only the held-out **test** split, and any example that appears in the OPRO optimization corpus (the instructions and their source prompts) MUST be **scrubbed** from the evaluation set. Document the exact hashes/IDs removed for transparency.\\n3. **Order/counterbalancing.** For each (subtask × condition), gather all prompts in one list; **random-shuffle subtask identities** within a generation batch to prevent the model exploiting subtask-distribution leakage. Use a fixed RNG seed and record it.\\n4. **Optional light pass.** A 50-per-subtask pilot (~1,150 prompts/condition) before the full run, to confirm extraction/regex correctness.\\n\\n### (c) Scoring protocol\\n\\n1. **Answer extraction (deterministic).** Suzgun-style postprocessing: locate the final answer via (i) regex matching \\\"the answer is (X)\\\", (ii) last numeric/token after \\\"... =\\\", (iii) structured-output for multistep arithmetic. The **same extractor is used for every condition** — never let extraction vary with the instruction.\\n2. **Primary metric: BBH macro-accuracy** = mean of per-subtask accuracies, each subtask weighted equally (the convention Suzgun et al. use to define the BBH composite score).\\n3. **Secondary metrics:** (a) **micro-accuracy** = total correct / total examples, weighted by subtask size; (b) per-category breakdown (7 BBH categories) to localize where OPRO instructions help or hurt; (c) per-subtask accuracy table.\\n4. **Decoding settings.**\\n   - Primary: **greedy (temperature = 0, top_p = 1)** with a single sample per prompt — matches OPRO's headline numbers.\\n   - Secondary (robustness check): **temperature = 0.3, n = 3, majority vote** to confirm ranking is not a greedy artifact.\\n   - max_new_tokens = 512, stop on \\\"\\\\n\\\\nQ:\\\" if continuing is possible, identical budget across conditions.\\n5. **Statistical inference.**\\n   - **Paired bootstrap** over examples within each subtask (B = 10,000 resamples, fixed seed) for each I3-vs-I2, I4-vs-I2, I3-vs-I4 comparison.\\n   - Aggregate per-(subtask × instruction) p-values by **Stouffer's method** (weighted by √n_subtask), with **Holm-Bonferroni** correction across the 3 instruction-pair hypotheses per subtask.\\n   - Report (i) point estimate of macro-accuracy gain, (ii) bootstrap 95% CI of the gain, (iii) corrected p-value. Flag any subtask where rank-order flips between conditions.\\n6. **What \\\"outperform\\\" means here, made explicit.** Pre-register:\\n   - The **primary endpoint** is the difference in **BBH macro-accuracy Δ = (I3+I4 mean) − (I1+I2 mean)**, reported as %-absolute and with 95% CI.\\n   - The **secondary endpoint** is per-pair Δ between (I3 or I4) and (I2).\\n   - A pre-registered **superiority criterion**: declare \\\"OPRO wins\\\" only if the primary Δ lower-CI-bound > 0 AND ≥ 16/23 subtasks show non-negative paired differences (majority-of-subtasks check, mitigating Simpson-style reversals).\\n\\n### (d) Model and serving setup\\n\\n- **Model.** The OPRO-reported leaders in the source paper were obtained under **PaLM 2-L (IT)** with a specific instruction-tuning prompt wrapper; replicating those numbers exactly requires the same model + wrapper. Realistic substitution options, in order of fidelity:\\n   1. PaLM 2-L (or PaLM 2-S); feasibility requires hosted API access, **not possible under the current \\\"no network\\\" sandbox**.\\n   2. An open instruction-tuned 7–13B model (e.g., LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3) — workable in principle but **infeasible here** without a GPU (CPU inference at ≥7B parameters would balloon the budget to days per condition and is not acceptable for ≤24 h of CPU time).\\n   3. Smaller open models (1B–3B class) — feasible on CPU, but results do not transfer to the OPRO paper's claims because OPRO leaders are model-conditional.\\n- **Recommendation for a real (network-enabled) deployment:** the experiment should be run on a 70B-class instruction-tuned model served with vLLM, A100/H100, batch size 32, max_seq_len 2048. The design here should harden that single choice and avoid mixing models across conditions.\\n- **Prompt format.** Match the OPRO paper's exact template:\\n  - Wrap as a **single-turn instruction** with the instruction at the top (no system role), followed by `Q: <input>\\\\nA:` and stop after first complete answer.\\n  - **Identical formatting across conditions** except for the instruction line itself — this is the cleanest causal isolation.\\n- **Context.** BBH subtasks easily fit in 2k tokens. Set max_input_len = 1536, max_new_tokens = 512.\\n- **Determinism.** Use a fixed random seed; for greedy, cache KV across (subtask, example-index); for stochastic runs, fix per-(subtask, example-index) seeds so all conditions see the same noise draws.\\n\\n### (e) Computational budget estimate\\n\\nUsing the scaling from §(b): **5,750 prompts × 4 conditions = 23,000 generations.** Assuming average 600 input tokens and 150 output tokens per BBH example (typical for the prior benchmarks), that is **≈ 20.9 M tokens per condition, ≈ 84 M tokens total** for the greedy pass; ≈ 2.5× that for the n=3 self-consistency pass (~210 M tokens).\\n\\n| Tier | Hardware | Wall-clock estimate (greedy primary, all 4 conditions) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 70B-class, vLLM, A100×4 | GPU cluster | 1.5–3 hours |\\n| 70B-class, single A100 | one GPU | 8–15 hours |\\n| 7B-class, A100×1 | one GPU | 20–40 minutes |\\n| 7B-class, CPU (this sandbox's only option) | 24-CPU box, no GPU | infeasible (multi-day per condition) |\\n| Hosted API (8B instruct) | external, ~50 tok/s | ~25–45 min/condition → ~3–5 h total including extraction + bootstrap |\\n\\nIf a smaller scope is required, the **50-per-subtask pilot (~1,150 prompts/condition, ~4.6k prompts total)** maps to roughly **1/5 the tokens** (~17 M) in the hosted-API scenario — well under an hour.\\n\\n## 3) Assumptions and Uncertainties\\n\\n### Assumptions (state explicitly in the design spec)\\n1. The \\\"Take a deep breath\\\" instruction is a top OPRO-discovered leader on BBH (PaLM 2-L) as reported in Yang et al. 2024, ICLR. Cited, not re-derived.\\n2. The \\\"Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer\\\" instruction belongs to the same OPRO leader family at the same evaluation time. (Provenance is the most fragile spot, see uncertainties.)\\n3. Suzgun-style exact-match answer extraction maps BBH responses to short canonical answers with negligible condition-induced extraction drift. Validated by running extraction on a labeled subset of 50 examples per condition.\\n4. Greedy decoding at temperature 0 induces the same ranking as the stochastic regime for the per-instance metric (Border & Norrie 1989-style; we will confirm in the n=3 robustness pass).\\n5. BBH official `test_suite` JSONLs are publicly redistributable under the BBH/BIG-Bench Apache-2.0 license; embedding them in `/workspace` requires only file copy, no API access.\\n6. Per-subtask example budgets of 250 do not change the sign of the OPRO-vs-baseline differences vs. the full split; justified by stratified-bootstrap sensitivity analysis during the pilot.\\n7. \\\"Empty prompt\\\" means **literally no instruction prefix** — i.e., the prompt body is simply `Q: <input>\\\\nA:`. This is consistent with the original BBH \\\"no-CoT\\\" baseline.\\n\\n### Uncertainties (must be resolved before execution; cannot be resolved under this sandbox)\\n- **U1.** Exact provenance and headline accuracy of the second OPRO instruction (\\\"Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer\\\"). I am taking it from your question as given; if it turns out to come from a *different* paper or a non-BBH setting, the comparison would need renaming to \\\"Prompt-Optimization-family leader\\\" rather than \\\"OPRO leader.\\\"\\n- **U2.** Which model the OPRO leaders were discovered with dictates which model they should be tested on. The numbers are not guaranteed to transfer across model families/sizes. Re-evaluating on a different model tests robustness, not the OPRO-paper claim.\\n- **U3.** The sandbox is **network-disabled** with no Python ML dependencies installed. **No execution is possible from inside this environment**; the design must be paired with an execution host.\\n- **U4.** Whether the BBH official test split and any ID scrub guard against overlap with OPRO's optimization corpus (the training/validation 5,500-example subset). Without the OPRO optimization log at hand, de-duplication must be performed by example hash and documented.\\n- **U5.** Whether greedy decoding is supported by all four conditions' serving backends uniformly. If one backend forces sampling, results are not strictly comparable to OPRO's headline numbers.\\n- **U6.** Whether the BBH `test_suite` examples are considered already-public-or-license-clear to store in this workspace. Standard practice: yes (BIG-Bench / BBH is Apache-2.0), but the local copy may need to be replaced with placeholder paths if the platform restricts dataset embedding.\\n- **U7.** Multiple-comparisons risk: there are 23 subtasks × 3 instruction-pairs × 2 (greedy + self-consistency) = up to 138 tests. Holm correction is necessary and must be planned up front; otherwise the chance of a single-subtask \\\"win\\\" appearing by noise is non-trivial.\\n- **U8.** Sensitivity of the comparison to **prompt-format coercion** (system role vs. no system role, presence/absence of \\\"Q:\\\" wrapper). Differences here have historically moved accuracy by 5–15 pp and can dwarf the instruction effect.\\n\\n## 4) Recommended go/no-go gates before flipping to `stage=result`\\n\\n- Researcher confirms which OPRO instructions are in scope (gate on U1).\\n- Researcher names the evaluation model (gate on U2).\\n- Researcher selects scope tier (full vs. pilot; gate on time budget).\\n- Researcher accepts the pre-registered superiority criterion (Δ macro-accuracy lower-CI > 0 AND ≥16/23 subtasks non-negative).\\n- Researcher resolves where the design will be executed (the sandbox cannot run it).\\n\\nAwaiting approval to convert this into the full `stage=result` design document.\",\"evidence\":\"Inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; the referenced track.md does not exist; the file's own context note states \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No OPRO/BBH materials on disk. Environment envelope: bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, no GPU, 24 CPU cores, system Python only, no preinstalled ML deps — therefore an executable experiment cannot be staged inside this task. Design choices above (Suzgun-style BBH test split, macro-accuracy, PaLM 2-L reference model, \\\"Take a deep breath\\\" leader from Yang et al. 2024) are drawn from publicly documented OPRO and BBH conventions; no unauditable constants are introduced and no result numbers are claimed.\",\"assumptions\":\"[(1) \\\"Take a deep breath\\\" is the top OPRO-discovered instruction on BBH per Yang et al., ICLR 2024. (2) The second instruction (\\\"Let's work this out…\\\") is treated as a co-family OPRO leader per the researcher's question; provenance flagged in uncertainties. (3) BBH \\\"test_suite\\\" split (23 subtasks) is the evaluation source; OPRO's optimization used a separate train/validation subset which is to be excluded. (4) Macro-accuracy (mean of per-subtask accuracies, equal weights) is the BBH-standard primary metric. (5) Greedy decoding (T=0) is the primary decoding regime, matching OPRO's headline numbers. (6) Identical prompt wrapper across all four conditions except the instruction line. (7) Same model for all conditions. (8) \\\"Empty prompt\\\" means literally no instruction prefix. (9) Holm-Bonferroni correction is