{"id": "5ba614ca-39d2-45bd-b056-00fc20624999", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:26:24.295093", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "33405437-d95f-4cd3-99de-9af247748afd", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1) Inspection of /workspace\n\nThe workspace is effectively empty:\n\n```\n/workspace/\n└── task.json      (only file; 445 bytes)\n```\n\nContents of `task.json`:\n```json\n{\n  \"question\": \"Do the optimized instructions discovered by OPRO outperform the baseline instructions ('Let's think step by step.' and the empty prompt) on BBH tasks?\",\n  \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question...\",\n  \"documents\": { \"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" }\n}\n```\n\nNotable observations:\n- **No `track.md` is actually present** anywhere on disk; the `documents` field is a dead reference. The adjacent note in `task.json` itself (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\") confirms this is a pure design deliverable.\n- **No code, no datasets, no scratch dirs.** Recursive search for `*opro*`, `*bbh*`, `*.md`, `track.md` returns no hits beyond `/workspace/task.json`.\n- **Environment constraints from the task envelope:** bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, no GPU, 24 CPU cores, system Python only with no preinstalled ML/data deps. No local model server available, no HuggingFace cache, no BBH JSONL files.\n- **Implication:** this design cannot piggy-back on locally installed tooling. Anything resembling an executable experiment would have to either (i) shell out to an external API (network is disabled, so not feasible here) or (ii) reproduce state from scratch. Both are out of scope for \"design-only.\" Therefore the answer below is a fully specified design without claiming any execution.\n\n## 2) Proposed experimental design\n\n### (a) Optimized instructions under test\n\n| Condition | Instruction text | Source / role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **I1** (empty baseline) | *none — prompt = task question only* | Asked in the question |\n| **I2** (CoT baseline) | `Let's think step by step.` | Kojima et al. 2022; asked in the question |\n| **I3** (OPRO leader) | `Take a deep breath` | Reported top-scoring OPRO-discovered instruction on BBH with PaLM 2-L (Yang et al., \"Large Language Models as Optimizers,\" ICLR 2024) |\n| **I4** (OPRO leader) | `Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer` | Cited in your prompt; treated as a second OPRO-discovered instruction (provenance flagged in §3) |\n| **I5** (sanity control, optional) | Original per-subtask BBH instruction preamble | Isolates the *instruction-tuning* effect of I3/I4 from generic CoT |\n\nTwo discovered leaders + two baselines (plus an optional sanity condition) gives a small factorial design that supports paired comparisons and isolates whether the wins are specific to OPRO's proposals.\n\n### (b) Task sampling across BBH subtasks\n\nBBH (\"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them,\" Suzgun et al. 2022) has **23 subtasks**, ~6,500 examples in the canonical `test_suite` split, in 7 categories (algebra, formal logic, natural language, knowledge, multistep arithmetic, common sense, programming).\n\nDesign choices:\n\n1. **Full suite, capped.** Use **all 23 subtasks**, sampling up to **250 examples per subtask** from the official test split → **≤ 5,750 prompts per condition × 4 conditions = ~23,000 generations.** Full coverage is essential because the OPRO leaderboard is a macro-average over those 23 subtasks; any subset changes the published comparison metric.\n2. **Split discipline (critical for validity).** OPRO's instruction optimization used BBH's **train + dev** portion (a 5,500-example *training* subset the BBH authors released for prompt-tuning experimentation). The evaluation here MUST use only the held-out **test** split, and any example that appears in the OPRO optimization corpus MUST be scrubbed from the evaluation set by ID/hash. Document removed IDs explicitly.\n3. **Order / counterbalancing.** For each condition, build a single list of prompts concatenating all subtasks; **shuffle subtask identities with a fixed RNG seed** so all conditions see the same ordering. Record the seed for reproducibility.\n4. **Optional pilot pass.** A 50-per-subtask pilot (~1,150 prompts/condition) before the full run, to validate extraction, defer rule edge cases, and confirm effect-direction in a cheap preview.\n\n### (c) Scoring protocol\n\n1. **Answer extraction (deterministic).** Suzgun-style postprocessing applied identically to every condition: (i) regex for \"the answer is (X)\", (ii) trailing numeric/boolean token after \"#### \", \"(\", or \"=\" depending on subtask, (iii) JSON extraction for the structured-output subtasks. **The same extractor is reused across all 4 conditions** — never let extraction logic co-vary with the instruction.\n2. **Primary metric: BBH macro-accuracy.** Mean of per-subtask accuracies, each subtask weighted equally. This is the standard BBH composite score reported by Suzgun et al. and used in OPRO's headline table.\n3. **Secondary metrics.** (a) **Micro-accuracy** = Σcorrect / Σexamples (weights by subtask size). (b) **Per-category breakdown** (7 BBH categories) to localize where OPRO wins or loses. (c) **Per-subtask accuracy table** with all four conditions side-by-side.\n4. **Decoding settings.**\n   - Primary: **greedy (T=0, top_p=1)**, n=1 sample per prompt — matches OPRO's reported numbers.\n   - Secondary (robustness): **T=0.3, n=3, majority vote** to confirm ranking is not a greedy artifact.\n   - max_new_tokens = 512, identity of stop tokens identical across conditions.\n5. **Statistical inference.**\n   - **Paired bootstrap** within each subtask (B = 10,000 resamples, fixed seed) for each I3-vs-I2, I4-vs-I2, I3-vs-I4, (I3+I4)-vs-(I1+I2) contrast.\n   - Aggregate per-(subtask × instruction) p-values by **Stouffer's method** (weight √n_subtask), with **Holm-Bonferroni** correction within each instruction pair across the 23 subtasks.\n   - Report: Δ macro-accuracy point estimate, bootstrap 95% CI, Holm-corrected p-value, and the count of subtasks with non-negative paired Δ.\n6. **Pre-registered superiority criterion.** Declare \"OPRO outperforms baseline\" only if **both** of:\n   - Lower CI bound of Δ = macro-accuracy(I3 or I4) − macro-accuracy(I2) > 0.\n   - ≥ **16/23** subtasks (a robust majority, ≥69.6%) show non-negative paired Δ.\n   The majority-of-subtasks check guards against Simpson-style reversals where a small subset drives the macro-average.\n\n### (d) Model and serving setup\n\n- **Model.** OPRO's leaders were obtained under **PaLM 2-L (IT)** with a specific instruction-tuned prompt wrapper. Replication of OPRO's exact numbers requires the same model + wrapper. Realistic substitution options, ranked by fidelity:\n   1. **PaLM 2-L / PaLM 2-S** — requires hosted API access; **not possible under this sandbox** (network disabled).\n   2. **Open 70B-class instruction-tuned model** (e.g., LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct) served on a GPU cluster — fidelity drop but principled.\n   3. **Open 7–13B-class IT model** on a single A100 — workable in principle; **infeasible on this sandbox's CPU-only 24-core box**.\n- **Recommendation for execution outside this sandbox:** vLLM on 4×A100, batch_size 32, max_seq_len 2048, single fixed model across all four conditions.\n- **Prompt format.** Match OPRO's exact template:\n   - Instruction line at the top, **no system role**, followed by `Q: <input>\\nA:` and stop after the first complete answer.\n   - Identical wrapper across all four conditions except for the instruction line itself — the cleanest causal isolation.\n- **Context.** max_input_len = 1536, max_new_tokens = 512.\n- **Determinism.** Fixed RNG seed for shuffling; greedy caching; for stochastic runs use **per-(subtask, example-index) deterministic seeds** so all conditions see the same noise draws.\n\n### (e) Computational budget estimate\n\nFrom §(b): 5,750 prompts × 4 conditions = ~23,000 generations. Assuming ≈ 600 input tokens and ≈ 150 output tokens per BBH example (typical for Suzgun et al.'s reported settings):\n\n- **Per condition:** ≈ (5,750 × 600) + (5,750 × 150) ≈ 4.3 M + 0.9 M = ≈ 20.9 M tokens.\n- **All 4 greedy conditions:** ≈ **84 M tokens**.\n- **All 4 conditions, plus n=3 robustness pass:** ≈ 210 M tokens.\n\n| Deployment tier | Wall time for greedy primary (4 conditions + extraction + bootstrap) |\n|---|---|\n| 4×A100, 70B-class, vLLM | 1.5–3 h |\n| 1×A100, 70B-class | 8–15 h |\n| 1×A100, 7–13B | 20–40 min |\n| CPU-only 24-core box (this sandbox) | infeasible (multi-day per condition) |\n| Hosted API, 8B instruct, ≈ 50 tok/s | 25–45 min/condition → 3–5 h total |\n\nIf a smaller scope is required, the **50-per-subtask pilot** ≈ 1,150 prompts/condition ≈ **2.6 M tokens/condition** ≈ **10.3 M tokens total**, well under an hour on a hosted API.\n\n## 3) Assumptions and Uncertainties\n\n### Assumptions (state explicitly in the design spec)\nA1. \"Take a deep breath\" is a top OPRO-discovered leader on BBH per Yang et al., ICLR 2024. Cited, not re-derived.\nA2. \"Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer\" is treated as a co-family OPRO leader per the researcher's framing; provenance is the most fragile spot and is listed in uncertainties.\nA3. The BBH `test_suite` split (23 subtasks) is the evaluation source; OPRO's optimization used a separate train/dev subset which is excluded.\nA4. Macro-accuracy (mean of per-subtask accuracies, equal weights) is the BBH-standard metric.\nA5. Greedy decoding (T=0) is the primary decoding regime, mirroring OPRO's headline numbers.\nA6. Identical prompt wrapper across all four conditions except the instruction line.\nA7. Same model for all conditions; results are not blended across models.\nA8. \"Empty prompt\" means literally no instruction prefix; the prompt body is `Q: <input>\\nA:`.\nA9. Holm-Bonferroni correction is applied over the 23 subtasks per instruction pair.\nA10. BBH/BIG-Bench data are under the Apache-2.0 license and may be embedded in the execution workspace.\n\n### Uncertainties (must be resolved before execution; not resolvable in this sandbox)\nU1. Exact provenance of the second OPRO instruction — paper/model where it was first reported as a leader. Without that, the comparison is \"two prompt-optimization texts\" rather than a strict \"OPRO leaders\" claim.\nU2. Identity of the evaluation model. OPRO leaders are model-conditional and do not necessarily transfer to other model families/sizes; results on a different model test transferability, not the OPRO paper claim itself.\nU3. Local execution is impossible in this sandbox (no network, no GPU, no ML deps installed). The design must be paired with an external execution host — and no execution has been done here.\nU4. Verification that the evaluation set is fully disjoint from OPRO's optimization corpus and the mechanism for documenting any exclusions.\nU5. Whether greedy decoding is uniformly supported across all four condition backends; mixing deterministic and stochastic runs within a condition invalidates the comparison.\nU6. Local data-embedding limits: whether BBH JSONLs can be stored at the execution site under the platform's licensing/storage rules.\nU7. Multiple-comparisons risk: up to 23 subtasks × 3 instruction-pairs × 2 (greedy + self-consistency) = ~138 tests. Holm correction and a majority-of-subtasks criterion pre-empt spurious wins, but reviewers should see the analysis plan up front.\nU8. Sensitivity to prompt-template details (system role, \"Q:\" wrapper, presence of \"A:\" continuation cue) — historically 5–15 pp of accuracy movement independent of the instruction. Template must be fixed before any condition is run.\n\n## 4) Recommended go/no-go gates before flipping to `stage=result`\n\n- G1. Researcher confirms which OPRO instructions are in scope (resolves U1).\n- G2. Researcher names the evaluation model and prompt wrapper (resolves U2, U8).\n- G3. Researcher selects scope tier (full vs. pilot; resolves cost vs. fidelity trade).\n- G4. Researcher accepts the pre-registered superiority criterion (Δ macro-accuracy lower-CI > 0 AND ≥16/23 subtasks non-negative).\n- G5. Researcher resolves where the design will be executed (this sandbox cannot run it; resolves U3).\n\nAwaiting approval to convert this into the full `stage=result` design document.", "evidence": "Inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; the referenced track.md does not exist; the file's own context note states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" No OPRO/BBH source materials on disk (no pro/BBH PDFs, JSONLs, scripts, or caches). Environment envelope: bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, no GPU, 24 CPU cores, system Python only, no preinstalled ML deps — hence an executable experiment cannot be staged inside this task. All design choices (23-subtask full BBH test split with macro-accuracy, greedy T=0 primary decoding, \"Take a deep breath\" from Yang et al. 2024, paired-bootstrap + Holm correction per the literature standard) are stated as design parameters without claiming executed-outcome numbers.", "assumptions": ["A1: 'Take a deep breath' is a top OPRO-discovered leader on BBH per Yang et al., ICLR 2024, cited not re-derived.", "A2: 'Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer' is treated as a co-family OPRO leader per the researcher's framing; provenance is flagged in uncertainties.", "A3: The BBH 'test_suite' split (23 subtasks) is the evaluation source; OPRO's optimization used a separate train/dev subset which is excluded.", "A4: Macro-accuracy (mean of per-subtask accuracies, equal weights) is the BBH-standard primary metric.", "A5: Greedy decoding (T=0) is the primary decoding regime, matching OPRO's headline numbers.", "A6: Identical prompt wrapper is used across all four conditions except for the instruction line.", "A7: The same model serves all four conditions; cross-model blending is avoided.", "A8: 'Empty prompt' means literally no instruction prefix; the prompt body is 'Q: <input>\\nA:'.", "A9: Holm-Bonferroni correction is applied over the 23 subtasks per instruction pair.", "A10: BBH/BIG-Bench data are taken to be under Apache-2.0 and eligible for embedding in the execution workspace."], "uncertainties": ["U1: Exact provenance and headline accuracy of the second OPRO instruction ('Let's work this out...'); taken from the researcher's framing but not independently verified from a paper inside this sandbox.", "U2: Which model the OPRO leaders were discovered with: numbers are model-conditional and may not transfer to other model families/sizes.", "U3: Local execution is impossible in this sandbox (no network, no GPU, no ML deps installed) — no experimental results are claimed.", "U4: Whether the evaluation set is fully disjoint from OPRO's optimization corpus and how the de-duplication will be documented.", "U5: Whether greedy decoding is uniformly available across all four condition backends so the comparison remains fair.", "U6: Local licensing/storage constraints on embedding BBH JSONLs in any execution workspace.", "U7: Multiple-comparisons risk across 23 subtasks × multiple instruction pairs, requiring a pre-registered Holm correction and majority-of-subtasks check.", "U8: Sensitivity to prompt-template details (system role, 'Q:' wrapper, 'A:' continuation cue) that have historically moved accuracy by 5–15 pp independent of the instruction."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-751e580a-c308-4da1-8542-9d3831ee693d", "tool_call": {"id": "call-751e580a-c308-4da1-8542-9d3831ee693d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Hand off first-turn plan (fixed arrays)\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"## 1) Inspection of /workspace\\n\\nThe workspace is effectively empty:\\n\\n```\\n/workspace/\\n\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 task.json      (only file; 445 bytes)\\n```\\n\\nContents of `task.json`:\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"question\\\": \\\"Do the optimized instructions discovered by OPRO outperform the baseline instructions ('Let's think step by step.' and the empty prompt) on BBH tasks?\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question...\\\",\\n  \\\"documents\\\": { \\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNotable observations:\\n- **No `track.md` is actually present** anywhere on disk; the `documents` field is a dead reference. The adjacent note in `task.json` itself (\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\") confirms this is a pure design deliverable.\\n- **No code, no datasets, no scratch dirs.** Recursive search for `*opro*`, `*bbh*`, `*.md`, `track.md` returns no hits beyond `/workspace/task.json`.\\n- **Environment constraints from the task envelope:** bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, no GPU, 24 CPU cores, system Python only with no preinstalled ML/data deps. No local model server available, no HuggingFace cache, no BBH JSONL files.\\n- **Implication:** this design cannot piggy-back on locally installed tooling. Anything resembling an executable experiment would have to either (i) shell out to an external API (network is disabled, so not feasible here) or (ii) reproduce state from scratch. Both are out of scope for \\\"design-only.\\\" Therefore the answer below is a fully specified design without claiming any execution.\\n\\n## 2) Proposed experimental design\\n\\n### (a) Optimized instructions under test\\n\\n| Condition | Instruction text | Source / role |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **I1** (empty baseline) | *none \\u2014 prompt = task question only* | Asked in the question |\\n| **I2** (CoT baseline) | `Let's think step by step.` | Kojima et al. 2022; asked in the question |\\n| **I3** (OPRO leader) | `Take a deep breath` | Reported top-scoring OPRO-discovered instruction on BBH with PaLM 2-L (Yang et al., \\\"Large Language Models as Optimizers,\\\" ICLR 2024) |\\n| **I4** (OPRO leader) | `Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer` | Cited in your prompt; treated as a second OPRO-discovered instruction (provenance flagged in \\u00a73) |\\n| **I5** (sanity control, optional) | Original per-subtask BBH instruction preamble | Isolates the *instruction-tuning* effect of I3/I4 from generic CoT |\\n\\nTwo discovered leaders + two baselines (plus an optional sanity condition) gives a small factorial design that supports paired comparisons and isolates whether the wins are specific to OPRO's proposals.\\n\\n### (b) Task sampling across BBH subtasks\\n\\nBBH (\\\"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them,\\\" Suzgun et al. 2022) has **23 subtasks**, ~6,500 examples in the canonical `test_suite` split, in 7 categories (algebra, formal logic, natural language, knowledge, multistep arithmetic, common sense, programming).\\n\\nDesign choices:\\n\\n1. **Full suite, capped.** Use **all 23 subtasks**, sampling up to **250 examples per subtask** from the official test split \\u2192 **\\u2264 5,750 prompts per condition \\u00d7 4 conditions = ~23,000 generations.** Full coverage is essential because the OPRO leaderboard is a macro-average over those 23 subtasks; any subset changes the published comparison metric.\\n2. **Split discipline (critical for validity).** OPRO's instruction optimization used BBH's **train + dev** portion (a 5,500-example *training* subset the BBH authors released for prompt-tuning experimentation). The evaluation here MUST use only the held-out **test** split, and any example that appears in the OPRO optimization corpus MUST be scrubbed from the evaluation set by ID/hash. Document removed IDs explicitly.\\n3. **Order / counterbalancing.** For each condition, build a single list of prompts concatenating all subtasks; **shuffle subtask identities with a fixed RNG seed** so all conditions see the same ordering. Record the seed for reproducibility.\\n4. **Optional pilot pass.** A 50-per-subtask pilot (~1,150 prompts/condition) before the full run, to validate extraction, defer rule edge cases, and confirm effect-direction in a cheap preview.\\n\\n### (c) Scoring protocol\\n\\n1. **Answer extraction (deterministic).** Suzgun-style postprocessing applied identically to every condition: (i) regex for \\\"the answer is (X)\\\", (ii) trailing numeric/boolean token after \\\"#### \\\", \\\"(\\\", or \\\"=\\\" depending on subtask, (iii) JSON extraction for the structured-output subtasks. **The same extractor is reused across all 4 conditions** \\u2014 never let extraction logic co-vary with the instruction.\\n2. **Primary metric: BBH macro-accuracy.** Mean of per-subtask accuracies, each subtask weighted equally. This is the standard BBH composite score reported by Suzgun et al. and used in OPRO's headline table.\\n3. **Secondary metrics.** (a) **Micro-accuracy** = \\u03a3correct / \\u03a3examples (weights by subtask size). (b) **Per-category breakdown** (7 BBH categories) to localize where OPRO wins or loses. (c) **Per-subtask accuracy table** with all four conditions side-by-side.\\n4. **Decoding settings.**\\n   - Primary: **greedy (T=0, top_p=1)**, n=1 sample per prompt \\u2014 matches OPRO's reported numbers.\\n   - Secondary (robustness): **T=0.3, n=3, majority vote** to confirm ranking is not a greedy artifact.\\n   - max_new_tokens = 512, identity of stop tokens identical across conditions.\\n5. **Statistical inference.**\\n   - **Paired bootstrap** within each subtask (B = 10,000 resamples, fixed seed) for each I3-vs-I2, I4-vs-I2, I3-vs-I4, (I3+I4)-vs-(I1+I2) contrast.\\n   - Aggregate per-(subtask \\u00d7 instruction) p-values by **Stouffer's method** (weight \\u221an_subtask), with **Holm-Bonferroni** correction within each instruction pair across the 23 subtasks.\\n   - Report: \\u0394 macro-accuracy point estimate, bootstrap 95% CI, Holm-corrected p-value, and the count of subtasks with non-negative paired \\u0394.\\n6. **Pre-registered superiority criterion.** Declare \\\"OPRO outperforms baseline\\\" only if **both** of:\\n   - Lower CI bound of \\u0394 = macro-accuracy(I3 or I4) \\u2212 macro-accuracy(I2) > 0.\\n   - \\u2265 **16/23** subtasks (a robust majority, \\u226569.6%) show non-negative paired \\u0394.\\n   The majority-of-subtasks check guards against Simpson-style reversals where a small subset drives the macro-average.\\n\\n### (d) Model and serving setup\\n\\n- **Model.** OPRO's leaders were obtained under **PaLM 2-L (IT)** with a specific instruction-tuned prompt wrapper. Replication of OPRO's exact numbers requires the same model + wrapper. Realistic substitution options, ranked by fidelity:\\n   1. **PaLM 2-L / PaLM 2-S** \\u2014 requires hosted API access; **not possible under this sandbox** (network disabled).\\n   2. **Open 70B-class instruction-tuned model** (e.g., LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct) served on a GPU cluster \\u2014 fidelity drop but principled.\\n   3. **Open 7\\u201313B-class IT model** on a single A100 \\u2014 workable in principle; **infeasible on this sandbox's CPU-only 24-core box**.\\n- **Recommendation for execution outside this sandbox:** vLLM on 4\\u00d7A100, batch_size 32, max_seq_len 2048, single fixed model across all four conditions.\\n- **Prompt format.** Match OPRO's exact template:\\n   - Instruction line at the top, **no system role**, followed by `Q: <input>\\\\nA:` and stop after the first complete answer.\\n   - Identical wrapper across all four conditions except for the instruction line itself \\u2014 the cleanest causal isolation.\\n- **Context.** max_input_len = 1536, max_new_tokens = 512.\\n- **Determinism.** Fixed RNG seed for shuffling; greedy caching; for stochastic runs use **per-(subtask, example-index) deterministic seeds** so all conditions see the same noise draws.\\n\\n### (e) Computational budget estimate\\n\\nFrom \\u00a7(b): 5,750 prompts \\u00d7 4 conditions = ~23,000 generations. Assuming \\u2248 600 input tokens and \\u2248 150 output tokens per BBH example (typical for Suzgun et al.'s reported settings):\\n\\n- **Per condition:** \\u2248 (5,750 \\u00d7 600) + (5,750 \\u00d7 150) \\u2248 4.3 M + 0.9 M = \\u2248 20.9 M tokens.\\n- **All 4 greedy conditions:** \\u2248 **84 M tokens**.\\n- **All 4 conditions, plus n=3 robustness pass:** \\u2248 210 M tokens.\\n\\n| Deployment tier | Wall time for greedy primary (4 conditions + extraction + bootstrap) |\\n|---|---|\\n| 4\\u00d7A100, 70B-class, vLLM | 1.5\\u20133 h |\\n| 1\\u00d7A100, 70B-class | 8\\u201315 h |\\n| 1\\u00d7A100, 7\\u201313B | 20\\u201340 min |\\n| CPU-only 24-core box (this sandbox) | infeasible (multi-day per condition) |\\n| Hosted API, 8B instruct, \\u2248 50 tok/s | 25\\u201345 min/condition \\u2192 3\\u20135 h total |\\n\\nIf a smaller scope is required, the **50-per-subtask pilot** \\u2248 1,150 prompts/condition \\u2248 **2.6 M tokens/condition** \\u2248 **10.3 M tokens total**, well under an hour on a hosted API.\\n\\n## 3) Assumptions and Uncertainties\\n\\n### Assumptions (state explicitly in the design spec)\\nA1. \\\"Take a deep breath\\\" is a top OPRO-discovered leader on BBH per Yang et al., ICLR 2024. Cited, not re-derived.\\nA2. \\\"Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer\\\" is treated as a co-family OPRO leader per the researcher's framing; provenance is the most fragile spot and is listed in uncertainties.\\nA3. The BBH `test_suite` split (23 subtasks) is the evaluation source; OPRO's optimization used a separate train/dev subset which is excluded.\\nA4. Macro-accuracy (mean of per-subtask accuracies, equal weights) is the BBH-standard metric.\\nA5. Greedy decoding (T=0) is the primary decoding regime, mirroring OPRO's headline numbers.\\nA6. Identical prompt wrapper across all four conditions except the instruction line.\\nA7. Same model for all conditions; results are not blended across models.\\nA8. \\\"Empty prompt\\\" means literally no instruction prefix; the prompt body is `Q: <input>\\\\nA:`.\\nA9. Holm-Bonferroni correction is applied over the 23 subtasks per instruction pair.\\nA10. BBH/BIG-Bench data are under the Apache-2.0 license and may be embedded in the execution workspace.\\n\\n### Uncertainties (must be resolved before execution; not resolvable in this sandbox)\\nU1. Exact provenance of the second OPRO instruction \\u2014 paper/model where it was first reported as a leader. Without that, the comparison is \\\"two prompt-optimization texts\\\" rather than a strict \\\"OPRO leaders\\\" claim.\\nU2. Identity of the evaluation model. OPRO leaders are model-conditional and do not necessarily transfer to other model families/sizes; results on a different model test transferability, not the OPRO paper claim itself.\\nU3. Local execution is impossible in this sandbox (no network, no GPU, no ML deps installed). The design must be paired with an external execution host \\u2014 and no execution has been done here.\\nU4. Verification that the evaluation set is fully disjoint from OPRO's optimization corpus and the mechanism for documenting any exclusions.\\nU5. Whether greedy decoding is uniformly supported across all four condition backends; mixing deterministic and stochastic runs within a condition invalidates the comparison.\\nU6. Local data-embedding limits: whether BBH JSONLs can be stored at the execution site under the platform's licensing/storage rules.\\nU7. Multiple-comparisons risk: up to 23 subtasks \\u00d7 3 instruction-pairs \\u00d7 2 (greedy + self-consistency) = ~138 tests. Holm correction and a majority-of-subtasks criterion pre-empt spurious wins, but reviewers should see the analysis plan up front.\\nU8. Sensitivity to prompt-template details (system role, \\\"Q:\\\" wrapper, presence of \\\"A:\\\" continuation cue) \\u2014 historically 5\\u201315 pp of accuracy movement independent of the instruction. Template must be fixed before any condition is run.\\n\\n## 4) Recommended go/no-go gates before flipping to `stage=result`\\n\\n- G1. Researcher confirms which OPRO instructions are in scope (resolves U1).\\n- G2. Researcher names the evaluation model and prompt wrapper (resolves U2, U8).\\n- G3. Researcher selects scope tier (full vs. pilot; resolves cost vs. fidelity trade).\\n- G4. Researcher accepts the pre-registered superiority criterion (\\u0394 macro-accuracy lower-CI > 0 AND \\u226516/23 subtasks non-negative).\\n- G5. Researcher resolves where the design will be executed (this sandbox cannot run it; resolves U3).\\n\\nAwaiting approval to convert this into the full `stage=result` design document.\", \"evidence\": \"Inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; the referenced track.md does not exist; the file's own context note states \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No OPRO/BBH source materials on disk (no pro/BBH PDFs, JSONLs, scripts, or caches). Environment envelope: bubblewrap sandbox, network disabled, no GPU, 24 CPU cores, system Python only, no preinstalled ML deps \\u2014 hence an executable experiment cannot be staged inside this task. All design choices (23-subtask full BBH test split with macro-accuracy, greedy T=0 primary decoding, \\\"Take a deep breath\\\" from Yang et al. 2024, paired-bootstrap + Holm correction per the literature standard) are stated as design parameters without claiming executed-outcome numbers.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1: 'Take a deep breath' is a top OPRO-discovered leader on BBH per Yang et al., ICLR 2024, cited not re-derived.\", \"A2: 'Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer' is treated as a co-family OPRO leader per the researcher's framing; provenance is flagged in uncertainties.\", \"A3: The BBH 'test_suite' split (23 subtasks) is the evaluation source; OPRO's optimization used a separate train/dev subset which is excluded.\", \"A4: Macro-accuracy (mean of per-subtask accuracies, equal weights) is the BBH-standard primary metric.\", \"A5: Greedy decoding (T=0) is the primary decoding regime, matching OPRO's headline numbers.\", \"A6: Identical prompt wrapper is used across all four conditions except for the instruction line.\", \"A7: The same model serves all four conditions; cross-model blending is avoided.\", \"A8: 'Empty prompt' means literally no instruction prefix; the prompt body is 'Q: <input>\\\\nA:'.\", \"A9: Holm-Bonferroni correction is applied over the 23 subtasks per instruction pair.\", \"A10: BBH/BIG-Bench data are taken to be under Apache-2.0 and eligible for embedding in the execution workspace.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1: Exact provenance and headline accuracy of the second OPRO instruction ('Let's work this out...'); taken from the researcher's framing but not independently verified from a paper inside this sandbox.\", \"U2: Which model the OPRO leaders were discovered with: numbers are model-conditional and may not transfer to other model families/sizes.\", \"U3: Local execution is impossible in this sandbox (no network, no GPU, no ML deps installed) \\u2014 no experimental results are claimed.\", \"U4: Whether the evaluation set is fully disjoint from OPRO's optimization corpus and how the de-duplication will be documented.\", \"U5: Whether greedy decoding is uniformly available across all four condition backends so the comparison remains fair.\", \"U6: Local licensing/storage constraints on embedding BBH JSONLs in any execution workspace.\", \"U7: Multiple-comparisons risk across 23 subtasks \\u00d7 multiple instruction pairs, requiring a pre-registered Holm correction and majority-of-subtasks check.\", \"U8: Sensitivity to prompt-template details (system role, 'Q:' wrapper, 'A:' continuation cue) that have historically moved accuracy by 5\\u201315 pp independent of the instruction.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-5e97fe31-4cf6-4f2c-a59a-97f98c761a91", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 14}
