{"id": "39414a12-4efa-4cca-85d0-d54a1f3e1177", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:26:40.381744", "source": "user", "parent_id": "40847d5c-40b6-47b7-9f3b-112f104a6df6", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. The 4-condition factorial (empty + CoT + 2 OPRO leaders + optional sanity), held-out BBH test_suite split with OPRO-train/dev scrubbing, identical prompt wrapper across conditions, macro-accuracy as primary metric, paired bootstrap with Holm correction, and the dual-gate superiority criterion (lower-CI > 0 AND >=16/23 subtasks non-negative) are accepted as the backbone of the design. Sandbox infeasibility is correctly flagged and no executed results are claimed. Convert this into the final design document with the following tightening baked in: (1) State explicitly up front that, absent confirmed PaLM 2-L access, the experiment tests TRANSFERABILITY of OPRO leaders to a substitute model (e.g., LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct), NOT replication of OPRO's headline numbers; pick a named default substitute model and justify it. (2) Resolve U1 by either (a) confirming the second instruction is an OPRO-discovered leader from Yang et al. 2024 with the table/finding it comes from, or (b) dropping it and clearly stating only one OPRO leader ('Take a deep breath') is in scope. (3) Promote the pre-registered criterion, the metric, the primary contrast set, and the decoding regime to a 'Decision rules' section near the top so the document is self-contained. (4) In feasibility, present a tiered budget table (PaLM 2 hosted / 4xA100 70B / single A100 70B / 7-13B / API tier) with wall-time and cost-per-condition estimates, and call out the sandbox tier as 'infeasible'. (5) Add a brief 'Why these controls suffice' subsection explaining how empty + CoT + sanity isolate (i) instruction effect, (ii) generic CoT effect, (iii) instruction-tuning artefact. (6) Include a one-page checklist pre-execution that resolves G1-G5. Do not fabricate empirical outcomes. Return the final design document.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
{"id": "59f19293-806f-45b2-8f84-ab74a0e5af3a", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:28:04.013358", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "39414a12-4efa-4cca-85d0-d54a1f3e1177", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Final Experiment Design\n\n**Question.** Do the optimized instructions discovered by OPRO outperform the baseline instructions (\"Let's think step by step.\" and the empty prompt) on BBH tasks?\n\n**Status.** Design-only. No experimental results have been produced. No numbers in this document are claimed to be measured. Where a constant is needed for an estimate (token counts, throughput figures), it is marked as an assumption drawn from prior BBH / OPRO reporting convention, not from a run in this workspace.\n\n**Framing — replication vs. transferability.** OPRO's published leaders were discovered and ranked against **PaLM 2-L** under a specific instruction-tuned prompt wrapper. Without confirmed access to PaLM 2-L, this study **cannot replicate** those headline numbers. Instead, it is framed as a **transferability study** of OPRO's \"Take a deep breath\" instruction onto a substitute open-weights instruction-tuned model. Treating re-evaluation on a different model as \"OPRO wins/loses on BBH\" would misrepresent the source claim; treating it as \"OPRO leaders transfer to model X on BBH\" is the honest comparison.\n\n**Default substitute model:** **LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct** served via vLLM. Justification: (i) open weights, (ii) instruction-tuned chat model with documented BBH behavior, (iii) capability tier comparable to PaLM 2-L, (iv) feasible on 4×A100, (v) public, making the study reproducible without gated API access. Other transferable substitutes (e.g., Mistral-Large-Instruct, Qwen-2.5-72B-Instruct) may be substituted in replications; the analysis below assumes LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct throughout.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Decision Rules (pre-registered)\n\n| Item | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| **Primary metric** | BBH macro-accuracy = simple mean of per-subtask accuracies, each subtask weighted equally. |\n| **Secondary metrics** | Micro-accuracy (weighted by subtask size); per-category accuracy across the 7 BBH categories; per-subtask accuracy table. |\n| **Primary contrast set** | (i) **OPRO vs. CoT:** Δ₁ = macro-acc(Take a deep breath) − macro-acc(Let's think step by step.) |\n|  | (ii) **OPRO vs. empty:** Δ₂ = macro-acc(Take a deep breath) − macro-acc(empty) |\n|  | (iii) **OPRO vs. weak reference:** Δ₃ = macro-acc(Take a deep breath) − mean(macro-acc(empty), macro-acc(Let's think step by step.)) |\n| **Decoding regime (primary)** | Greedy, **T = 0.0**, **top_p = 1.0**, **n_sample = 1**, max_new_tokens = 512. Mirrors OPRO's headline numbers. |\n| **Decoding regime (robustness)** | **T = 0.3, n = 3, majority vote** under a **fixed per-(subtask, example-index) seed schedule** so all four conditions see the same noise draws. |\n| **Statistical procedure** | Per-subtask paired bootstrap over examples (10,000 resamples, fixed seed) for each contrast; aggregate p-values across the 23 subtasks by **Stouffer's method (weight √n_subtask)**; **Holm–Bonferroni** correction within each contrast family over the 23 subtasks. |\n| **Superiority criterion (dual gate)** | Declare *\"OPRO outperforms baseline on this BBH design\"* iff **both**: **(G1)** the **lower 95% bootstrap CI bound** of Δ₁ (and, separately, Δ₂) is **> 0**; **(G2)** ≥ **16/23** subtasks (a robust majority) show **non-negative** paired Δ in the OPRO condition. |\n| **Failure reporting** | If G1 passes but G2 fails → report **\"macro-average gain, majority-of-subtasks reversal\"**; if G1 fails → report **\"no detectable macro-accuracy gain\"**. Both null results pre-registered. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workspace & Environment Inspection (recap)\n\n- `/workspace/` contains **only `task.json`**; the referenced `track.md` is absent; `task.json` itself states *\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"*\n- No OPRO source paper PDF, no BBH JSONLs, no scripts, no caches on disk.\n- Sandbox envelope: bubblewrap, **network disabled**, **no GPU**, 24 CPU cores, system Python with **no preinstalled ML/eval/data dependencies**.\n- **Consequence:** execution (model serving, BBH file I/O, statistical bootstrap on real outputs) **cannot be staged from inside this sandbox.** All feasibility numbers in §7 are planning estimates against external deployment tiers; the sandbox tier is **infeasible**.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Conditions (with U1 resolved)\n\nPer the researcher's directive to resolve U1 honestly without fabricating paper-page citations:\n\n| Code | Instruction text | Role | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **I1** | *(none — prompt body is exactly `Q: <input>\\nA:`)* | No-instruction baseline | **In scope** (asked in the question) |\n| **I2** | `Let's think step by step.` | CoT baseline | **In scope** (asked in the question) |\n| **I3** | `Take a deep breath` | OPRO-discovered leader on BBH | **Sole OPRO leader in scope** (Yang et al., ICLR 2024, BBH section). |\n| **I4** | `Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer` | Instruction cited by researcher; **OPRO lineage not verifiable from materials available in this sandbox** | **Held in exploratory reserve.** Drop from primary contrasts. May be added only if the source paper + table are cited by the executing team before go/no-go. |\n| **I5 (optional)** | Original per-subtask BBH instruction preamble (the HTML original written by BBH authors) | Sanity reference | **Optional**, included if compute permits; isolates whether I3's effect is specifically OPRO-style rather than \"any well-tuned instruction\". |\n\nThe design therefore proceeds with **4 conditions mandatory (I1, I2, I3, plus one reserved slot)**; with I5 it becomes 5. Dropping I4 keeps the experiment's claim-space tight (\"does the OPRO-discovered leader beat CoT/empty on BBH?\") and avoids asserting a paper-table citation I cannot independently verify inside this sandbox.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Materials\n\n- **Dataset.** BIG-Bench Hard (`bbh`) released under the Apache-2.0 license by Suzgun et al. (2022). 23 subtasks, **test split** used here. OPRO/BBH authors release open JSONLs in the `bigbench/benchmark/bbh/<task>` directory of the `google/BIG-bench` repository; the executing team is to vendor these into the execution workspace **after** the licensing/storage gate in §10 is cleared.\n- **Split discipline.** OPRO's instruction optimization used BBH's **train + dev** split (the canonical \"training\" subset released for prompt-tuning experiments). Evaluation here uses **only `test_suite`**. Examples appearing in the OPRO optimization corpus are scrubbed by example-ID/hash; the **exclusion list** is published alongside the result.\n- **Model.** **LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct** (Meta; Llama 3 Community License). If the executing team selects a substitute (Mistral-Large-Instruct, Qwen-2.5-72B-Instruct, etc.) it must be declared **before** the run starts; cross-model blending is prohibited.\n- **Prompt wrapper (identical across all conditions).** Each generated continuation is conditioned on a single string of the form:\n\n  ```\n  <INSTRUCTION_LINE>\n  Q: <BBH input>\n  A:\n  ```\n\n  The `<INSTRUCTION_LINE>` is empty for I1, and is the literal instruction text for I2/I3/(I4/I5). **No system role. No few-shot examples. No chat templating tags.** This isolates the instruction-line manipulation as the sole treatment.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Sampling\n\n- **Coverage.** All 23 BBH subtasks; stratify by subtask.\n- **Per-subtask cap.** Up to **250 examples/subtask** from the held-out test split → ≤ **5,750 prompts/condition**.\n- **Order.** Concatenate all subtask prompts into a single list per condition; **shuffle with a fixed RNG seed** so all conditions see the same ordering. Record the seed.\n- **Pilot option (recommended).** 50 examples/subtask (~1,150 prompts/condition, 10× cheaper) to validate extraction and detect qualitatively opposite directions before committing to the full run.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Procedure\n\n1. **Vendor** BBH `test_suite` JSONLs into the execution workspace; cross-reference example IDs against the OPRO optimization train/dev list; publish an exclusion list and a final sample-count by subtask.\n2. **Build** the prompt wrapper for each (condition × subtask × example) triple under a fixed RNG seed; persist generation records (`example_id`, `condition`, `prompt`, `seed`).\n3. **Generate** continuations under the primary decoding regime (greedy, T=0); persist raw continuations. Caching of prompt encodings is encouraged; **no instruction-conditioned caching of the decode path**.\n4. **Extract** final answers with the deterministic extractor described in §8. Run a labeled-sample sanity pass on **50 random extractions** per condition; manually adjudicate any mismatch between extractor output and a human-readable correct answer; report **exact-match accuracy** under this extractor.\n5. *(Robustness)* repeat step 3 under the T=0.3, n=3, fixed-seed regime and apply **majority-of-3** to the extracted answers; record only the voted answer and the per-example vote distribution.\n6. **Score** with the metric set in §1; aggregate per-subtask → macro and micro accuracy → per-category.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Feasibility (Tiered Budget)\n\nToken accounting for the **full** primary pass (4 conditions × 5,750 prompts): ~5,750 × (≈600 input + ≈150 output) ≈ **20.9 M tokens/condition × 4 ≈ 84 M tokens** for greedy; ≈ **3× that** for the n=3 robustness pass (≈ 250 M tokens). Pilot tier (50/subtask) divides by ~5x.\n\n| # | Tier (model · serving) | Cost-tokens / condition | Wall-time per condition | Wall-time for full 4-condition primary | Cost notes | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 | **Sandbox:** LLaMA-3-70B on 24-CPU box, no GPU, no parallel inference framework | ≈ 20.9 M tokens | many **days/condition** with batch-CPU FP16/BF16 (≈ 0.3–0.5 tok/s/sample); up to ≈ 6 days/condition sustained | ≈ **3–4 weeks** for greedy primary alone | OPEX: electricity only | **Infeasible** (cannot stage here at all) |\n| T2 | LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct hosted (Fireworks / Together / OpenRouter) | ≈ 20.9 M tokens @ ~$1.2–1.8 /M tok | 25–45 min/condition @ 50 tok/s | ≈ 2–3 h greedy primary; ≈ 6–8 h incl. n=3 robustness | ≈ $100–$150 (4 conditions, gpt-budget) | **Default** |\n| T3 | LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct, **4× A100 / H100, vLLM** | ≈ 20.9 M tokens | 8–15 min @ ~250 tok/s/sample batch 32 | ≈ 35–60 min greedy primary | OPEX: cluster time | Excellent fidelity/cost |\n| T4 | LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct, **single A100/H100, vLLM** | ≈ 20.9 M tokens | 25–90 min | 1.5–6 h greedy primary | Includes batch_down | Workable; honors 24 h single-GPU constraint |\n| T5 | 7–13B-class IT (e.g., LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3), 1× A100 | ≈ 20.9 M tokens, 7B cost base | 20–40 min | **≠ 70B OOM at full size**; recompute on 70B-tier | Lower-**fidelity** (transferability drop) | Acceptable for transferability-first study if compute is tight |\n| T6 | PaLM 2-L (Vertex AI / MakerSuite) | ≈ 20.9 M tokens @ ~$1.0–1.4 /M tok | 25–35 min | 1.5–3 h | ≈ $90–$120 | Only meaningful **if** PlayerAccess **and** Vertex is in budget; otherwise skipped |\n\n**Recommendation.** **T3** is the safe production choice; **T2** is the best ROI when the executing team prefers managed inference; **T4** is room-and-board under tighter GPU constraints. **T1 (sandbox) is unusable** for this study on the current 24-CPU bubblewrap mount — the data point is included for transparency rather than to recommend.\n\n**Pilot option.** A 50-per-subtask pilot (T2) is ≈ 4.2 M tokens total → ≈ $5–$8 → 15–30 minutes wall clock; recommended as a gate before committing the full tier.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Scoring Protocol\n\n1. **Answer extraction (deterministic; same across all conditions).**\n   - Regex tier 1: `(?i)the answer is\\s+\\(?([-\\w/.,]+)\\)?` over the last 256 chars.\n   - Regex tier 2 (multistep arithmetic outputs): trailing token after `#### `, `=`, or final line tokens.\n   - Regex tier 3 (boolean / multiple-choice): exact-token match against the option set supplied by the subtask.\n   - Constrained-decoding tier: subtasks with fixed-output grammar (date understanding, web-of-lies, etc.) use a structured parser; reported separately so the comparison to free-form extractions is auditable.\n2. **Primary metric.** **Macro-accuracy** — simple mean of per-subtask accuracies over the 23 subtasks, equal weight per subtask. This is the score Suzgun et al. and Yang et al. report as the BBH composite.\n3. **Secondary metrics.** Micro-accuracy; per-category mean (algebra, formal logic, natural language, knowledge, multistep arithmetic, common sense, programming); per-subtask accuracy table.\n4. **Effect sizes.** Δ macro-accuracy expressed in absolute percentage points with bootstrap 95% CIs; per-subtask Δ and per-category Δ reported alongside.\n5. **Multiple runs.** The **primary** pass is **greedy, single sample**. The **robustness** pass is `T=0.3, n=3`, **majority vote** under a fixed per-(subtask, example-index) seed schedule; this is reported as a check on whether greedy rankings persist under sampling.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Statistical Analysis\n\n1. **Per-subtask paired bootstrap** for each (contrast × subtask): sample examples with replacement B=10,000 times from the **intersection** of examples scored by all conditions; compute Δ as macro-accuracy on the resample. Use a global RNG seed.\n2. **Cross-subtask aggregation** via **Stouffer's z-method** weighted by √n_subtask. **Holm–Bonferroni** correction within each contrast family across the 23 subtasks. Family-wise α = 0.05.\n3. **Bootstrap CIs on Δ macro-accuracy** at the **overall (BBH-wide) level**: pool bootstrap-resample per subtask, compute the macro-mean on the resample, report 2.5/97.5 percentile bounds.\n4. **Sign-test on subtasks**: count subtasks with non-negative Δ; compare to a binomial null (p=0.5) at Holm-corrected α=0.05. The **≥ 16/23 majority-of-subtasks gate (G2)** corresponds to p ≈ 0.046 (two-sided), which is Holm-consistent against the per-subtask Holm tier for a single primary contrast.\n5. **Sensitivity analyses (planned, not budget-required).**\n   - **Template perturbation:** re-run primary pass under a system-role + chat-template wrapper on **25% of the test split** (1,440 prompts/condition) to verify the rank order is not an artifact of the `Q:\\nA:` wrapper. (addresses U8).\n   - **Per-category stratification:** report Δ restricted to the 7 BBH categories to localize OPRO gains.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Why these controls suffice (isolation logic)\n\n| Possible confound | How it would bias a naive comparison | How this design isolates it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **(i) Instruction-text effect** (does *this literal instruction string* matter?) | Reporting only CoT vs. empty would attribute any improvement to \"instruction style\", not to OPRO specifically. | **I3 vs. I1** (instruction present vs. absent) quantifies the effect of *putting any instruction in the prompt*. **I3 vs. I2** quantifies whether OPRO's specific instruction beats the canonical CoT baseline. Taken together, these isolate the **specific-text contribution** of OPRO. |\n| **(ii) Generic CoT effect** (is \"Take a deep breath\" just a CoT-in-structural-form trigger?) | If \"Take a deep breath\" were a stealth CoT cue, I1/I2 alone would mis-attribute gains. | **I2** is the canonical \"Let's think step by step.\" CoT baseline, identical low-token-length instruction as the OPRO strings. The **Δ₁ contrast** is therefore CoT-style-vs-OPRO; a positive Δ₁ cannot be explained by generic \"instructions that prompt reasoning\" alone. |\n| **(iii) Instruction-tuning artifact** (does post-training on instruction-tuned data drive the gain?) | Some instruction-tuned chat models apply implicit rewriting to underspecified prompts. | The **identical prompt wrapper** and the **identical model** across all conditions guarantee the instruction-tuned behavior is constant. Any gain is therefore **inside the model, outside the wrapper** — i.e., a property of the instruction line itself. The optional **I5 (BBH original per-subtask instruction)** sanity reference further checks whether **any well-tuned instruction** matches I3, isolating whether I3 is better than \"the BBH authors' intended phrasing\". |\n| **(iv) Subtask-mix confound** | Different subtasks have very different ceilings (Penguins-in-a-Table vs. Multistep-Arithmetic). | **Macro-accuracy** weights subtasks equally; reported alongside **micro-accuracy** weights by example-count. The opposite-sign disagreement (if any) is itself a finding and is documented. |\n| **(v) Decoding/non-determinism confound** | Stochastic decoding can move accuracy by 5–10 pp within a model. | Primary pass is **T=0 greedy**. The robustness pass uses a **fixed-seed grid**, so any condition-vs-condition noise difference is upstream of the seed. |\n| **(vi) Selection / leakage from optimization corpus** | If BBH examples that fed OPRO's meta-search leak into the evaluation set, the comparison is biased. | Held-out **test split only**; explicit ID scrub against the OPRO train/dev IDs; exclusion list published. |\n\nNet: the design sets up **two sufficient contrasts** (I3 vs. I1 and I3 vs. I2) plus an **I1-vs-I2 sanity contrast**, with an identical wrapper and identical model. A win for OPRO is therefore a property of **the instruction string under fixed decoding on a single model**, holding wrapper/macro-mix/decoding/leakage constant.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Pre-execution Checklist (G1–G5)\n\nEach item is a yes/no gate; the executing team must initial each before launching the full pass.\n\n- **G1. Conditions locked.**\n  - [ ] I1 = empty, I2 = \"Let's think step by step.\", I3 = \"Take a deep breath\".\n  - [ ] I4 dropped from **primary** contrasts. **(U1 resolved.)**\n  - [ ] I5 (BBH original per-subtask instruction) = **off**, **on**, or **deferred post-pilot**. Default: **deferred**.\n\n- **G2. Model and template locked.** **(U2, U8)**\n  - [ ] Model = `LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct` (default). Substitute alternatives named and recorded.\n  - [ ] Prompt wrapper = `<INSTRUCTION_LINE>\\nQ: <input>\\nA:` with system role = none and no chat template tags.\n  - [ ] Decoding: T=0.0, top_p=1.0, n=1, max_new_tokens=512 for primary; T=0.3, n=3, fixed-seed grid for robustness.\n  - [ ] All four conditions (I1, I2, I3, [+I5]) share the wrapper verbatim.\n\n- **G3. Scope tier selected.** (Resolution from §7.)\n  - [ ] Tier = **T3 (4×A100, vLLM)** ← recommended, or **T2 (hosted)** ← budget-tight, or other.\n  - [ ] Pilot pass = **50/subtask** before the full pass (recommended).\n  - [ ] Wall-time + dollar figures recorded against the architecture choice.\n\n- **G4. Spread checks & pre-execution sanity.**\n  - [ ] BBH `test_suite` JSONLs vendored; example-ID scrub against OPRO train/dev list computed; **exclusion list finalized**.\n  - [ ] **50-prompt pilot run** succeeded across all conditions: each completion begins with valid English/structured output; idiom hallucination ≤ 5%; extraction works on ≥95% of pilot completions per condition.\n  - [ ] Same RNG seed across conditions; shuffling seed and per-(subtask, example-index) stochastic-seed schedule saved.\n  - [ ] Extraction tier assignment per subtask documented; controlled-output subtasks identified.\n\n- **G5. Analysis lock-down.**\n  - [ ] Statistical-analysis plan, including bootstrap seed, B=10,000, Stouffer weighting, and Holm–Bonferroni family structure, **committed before** the full pass; no opportunistic analyses added post-hoc.\n  - [ ] **Pre-registered superiority criterion** (lower-CI > 0 AND ≥16/23 subtasks non-negative) signed off and pasted into the final memo template.\n  - [ ] **Out-of-scope statements** acknowledged: no replication of OPRO's PaLM 2-L headline numbers is claimed; transferability-to-LLaMA-3-70B is the claim-space.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Out of Scope / Not Claimed\n\n- No claim is made that experiments have been run; no accuracy numbers appear in this document.\n- No claim is made about PaLM 2-L replication.\n- No claim that \"Let's work this out in a step by step way...\" is an OPRO-discovered leader without independent citation (it's held in exploratory reserve per §3).\n- No claim that this design, even if executed, would \"settle\" OPRO vs. all baselines under every model/decoding regime.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Final Memo Outline (post-execution; designed, not drafted)\n\nOnce executed, the team posts a memo in this order: **(1)** Decision rules (§1 above); **(2)** Conditions and sample counts per subtask post-scrub; **(3)** Primary pass table (I1/I2/I3/I5 — macro, micro, per-category); **(4)** Robustness pass table; **(5)** Δ table with bootstrap CIs and Holm-corrected p-values; **(6)** Sign test on subtasks; **(7)** Verdict keyed to the dual-gate criterion; **(8)** Limitations: model portability, wrapper-sensitivity, multiple-comparisons discipline.\n\n---\n*End of design document.*", "evidence": "Built on the approved first-turn plan. No empirical numbers invented; all feasibility figures are planning estimates against external deployment tiers, with the sandbox tier marked infeasible. U1 was resolved by dropping the second leader from primary scope (honest position the executing team can extend), G1–G5 fully mapped to a pre-execution checklist, decision rules promoted to the top, transferability framing (not replication) made explicit with LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct as the named default substitute, and an isolation-logic section added that shows how empty + CoT + (optional) original-instruction controls separate generic CoT, raw instruction, and instruction-tuning artifacts under fixed wrapper/decoding/model.", "assumptions": ["A1: 'Take a deep breath' is one of the top OPRO-discovered instructions on BBH per Yang et al., ICLR 2024; cited but not re-derived inside this sandbox.", "A2: 'Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer' is held in exploratory reserve pending independent provenance verification; the design's primary claims are scoped to A1.", "A3: BBH 'test_suite' split (23 subtasks) is the evaluation source; OPRO's optimization used a separate train/dev subset which is excluded by ID scrub.", "A4: Macro-accuracy (mean of per-subtask accuracies, equal weights) is the BBH-standard primary metric, matching Suzgun et al. and Yang et al.", "A5: Primary decoding is greedy (T=0); this mirrors OPRO's headline numbers but is a design choice the executing team must commit to.", "A6: The prompt wrapper `<INSTRUCTION_LINE>\\nQ: <input>\\nA:` is identical across all conditions; only the INSTRUCTION_LINE varies.", "A7: The same model (default LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct) serves all conditions; cross-model blending is not allowed.", "A8: 'Empty prompt' means no INSTRUCTION_LINE; the rest of the wrapper is shared.", "A9: Holm–Bonferroni correction operates within each contrast family over the 23 subtasks; Stouffer's method aggregates per-subtask bootstrap statistics with √n_subtask weights.", "A10: The 250-example/subtask cap is a planning parameter, not a statistic; the pilot pass should confirm it does not flip the sign of any contrast.", "A11: The token budget per example (≈600 input + ≈150 output) is a planning figure drawn from typical BBH prompt/output sizes as reported in the literature, not from local measurements.", "A12: The 16/23 majority-of-subtasks threshold aligns with a Holm-consistent binomial test against p=0.5 within the per-subtask Holm family for the primary contrast."], "uncertainties": ["U1: Provenance of the second instruction is unverified in this sandbox; resolved by dropping it from the primary contrasts and holding it in exploratory reserve.", "U2: Choice of substitute model affects transferability claims; substituting away from LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct induces a model-portability caveat.", "U3: The sandbox cannot execute this experiment (no network, no GPU, no ML deps); no result is claimed; feasibility refers to external execution tiers (T2–T6).", "U4: The scrub of OPRO train/dev IDs from the BBH evaluation set requires a copy of the OPRO optimization corpus at execution time; without it, the design's guarantee of leakage-freeness is partial.", "U5: Greedy decoding must be uniformly available across all backend alternatives; a backend that only supports sampling introduces a confounding input the design does not control for.", "U6: Local data-embedding rules at the execution site may require re-vendoring BBH separately from this workspace.", "U7: Multiple-comparisons discipline (per-subtask Holm) must be pre-registered; without it, the experiment is at higher false-discovery risk.", "U8: Prompt-template detail sensitivity is mitigated by the planned 25% template-perturbation sensitivity check on the test split."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-bd130882-50d0-4b91-848e-b3be2e8635fc", "tool_call": {"id": "call-bd130882-50d0-4b91-848e-b3be2e8635fc", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Hand off final design document\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Final Experiment Design\\n\\n**Question.** Do the optimized instructions discovered by OPRO outperform the baseline instructions (\\\"Let's think step by step.\\\" and the empty prompt) on BBH tasks?\\n\\n**Status.** Design-only. No experimental results have been produced. No numbers in this document are claimed to be measured. Where a constant is needed for an estimate (token counts, throughput figures), it is marked as an assumption drawn from prior BBH / OPRO reporting convention, not from a run in this workspace.\\n\\n**Framing \\u2014 replication vs. transferability.** OPRO's published leaders were discovered and ranked against **PaLM 2-L** under a specific instruction-tuned prompt wrapper. Without confirmed access to PaLM 2-L, this study **cannot replicate** those headline numbers. Instead, it is framed as a **transferability study** of OPRO's \\\"Take a deep breath\\\" instruction onto a substitute open-weights instruction-tuned model. Treating re-evaluation on a different model as \\\"OPRO wins/loses on BBH\\\" would misrepresent the source claim; treating it as \\\"OPRO leaders transfer to model X on BBH\\\" is the honest comparison.\\n\\n**Default substitute model:** **LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct** served via vLLM. Justification: (i) open weights, (ii) instruction-tuned chat model with documented BBH behavior, (iii) capability tier comparable to PaLM 2-L, (iv) feasible on 4\\u00d7A100, (v) public, making the study reproducible without gated API access. Other transferable substitutes (e.g., Mistral-Large-Instruct, Qwen-2.5-72B-Instruct) may be substituted in replications; the analysis below assumes LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct throughout.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Decision Rules (pre-registered)\\n\\n| Item | Rule |\\n|---|---|\\n| **Primary metric** | BBH macro-accuracy = simple mean of per-subtask accuracies, each subtask weighted equally. |\\n| **Secondary metrics** | Micro-accuracy (weighted by subtask size); per-category accuracy across the 7 BBH categories; per-subtask accuracy table. |\\n| **Primary contrast set** | (i) **OPRO vs. CoT:** \\u0394\\u2081 = macro-acc(Take a deep breath) \\u2212 macro-acc(Let's think step by step.) |\\n|  | (ii) **OPRO vs. empty:** \\u0394\\u2082 = macro-acc(Take a deep breath) \\u2212 macro-acc(empty) |\\n|  | (iii) **OPRO vs. weak reference:** \\u0394\\u2083 = macro-acc(Take a deep breath) \\u2212 mean(macro-acc(empty), macro-acc(Let's think step by step.)) |\\n| **Decoding regime (primary)** | Greedy, **T = 0.0**, **top_p = 1.0**, **n_sample = 1**, max_new_tokens = 512. Mirrors OPRO's headline numbers. |\\n| **Decoding regime (robustness)** | **T = 0.3, n = 3, majority vote** under a **fixed per-(subtask, example-index) seed schedule** so all four conditions see the same noise draws. |\\n| **Statistical procedure** | Per-subtask paired bootstrap over examples (10,000 resamples, fixed seed) for each contrast; aggregate p-values across the 23 subtasks by **Stouffer's method (weight \\u221an_subtask)**; **Holm\\u2013Bonferroni** correction within each contrast family over the 23 subtasks. |\\n| **Superiority criterion (dual gate)** | Declare *\\\"OPRO outperforms baseline on this BBH design\\\"* iff **both**: **(G1)** the **lower 95% bootstrap CI bound** of \\u0394\\u2081 (and, separately, \\u0394\\u2082) is **> 0**; **(G2)** \\u2265 **16/23** subtasks (a robust majority) show **non-negative** paired \\u0394 in the OPRO condition. |\\n| **Failure reporting** | If G1 passes but G2 fails \\u2192 report **\\\"macro-average gain, majority-of-subtasks reversal\\\"**; if G1 fails \\u2192 report **\\\"no detectable macro-accuracy gain\\\"**. Both null results pre-registered. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Workspace & Environment Inspection (recap)\\n\\n- `/workspace/` contains **only `task.json`**; the referenced `track.md` is absent; `task.json` itself states *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"*\\n- No OPRO source paper PDF, no BBH JSONLs, no scripts, no caches on disk.\\n- Sandbox envelope: bubblewrap, **network disabled**, **no GPU**, 24 CPU cores, system Python with **no preinstalled ML/eval/data dependencies**.\\n- **Consequence:** execution (model serving, BBH file I/O, statistical bootstrap on real outputs) **cannot be staged from inside this sandbox.** All feasibility numbers in \\u00a77 are planning estimates against external deployment tiers; the sandbox tier is **infeasible**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Conditions (with U1 resolved)\\n\\nPer the researcher's directive to resolve U1 honestly without fabricating paper-page citations:\\n\\n| Code | Instruction text | Role | Status |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **I1** | *(none \\u2014 prompt body is exactly `Q: <input>\\\\nA:`)* | No-instruction baseline | **In scope** (asked in the question) |\\n| **I2** | `Let's think step by step.` | CoT baseline | **In scope** (asked in the question) |\\n| **I3** | `Take a deep breath` | OPRO-discovered leader on BBH | **Sole OPRO leader in scope** (Yang et al., ICLR 2024, BBH section). |\\n| **I4** | `Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer` | Instruction cited by researcher; **OPRO lineage not verifiable from materials available in this sandbox** | **Held in exploratory reserve.** Drop from primary contrasts. May be added only if the source paper + table are cited by the executing team before go/no-go. |\\n| **I5 (optional)** | Original per-subtask BBH instruction preamble (the HTML original written by BBH authors) | Sanity reference | **Optional**, included if compute permits; isolates whether I3's effect is specifically OPRO-style rather than \\\"any well-tuned instruction\\\". |\\n\\nThe design therefore proceeds with **4 conditions mandatory (I1, I2, I3, plus one reserved slot)**; with I5 it becomes 5. Dropping I4 keeps the experiment's claim-space tight (\\\"does the OPRO-discovered leader beat CoT/empty on BBH?\\\") and avoids asserting a paper-table citation I cannot independently verify inside this sandbox.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Materials\\n\\n- **Dataset.** BIG-Bench Hard (`bbh`) released under the Apache-2.0 license by Suzgun et al. (2022). 23 subtasks, **test split** used here. OPRO/BBH authors release open JSONLs in the `bigbench/benchmark/bbh/<task>` directory of the `google/BIG-bench` repository; the executing team is to vendor these into the execution workspace **after** the licensing/storage gate in \\u00a710 is cleared.\\n- **Split discipline.** OPRO's instruction optimization used BBH's **train + dev** split (the canonical \\\"training\\\" subset released for prompt-tuning experiments). Evaluation here uses **only `test_suite`**. Examples appearing in the OPRO optimization corpus are scrubbed by example-ID/hash; the **exclusion list** is published alongside the result.\\n- **Model.** **LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct** (Meta; Llama 3 Community License). If the executing team selects a substitute (Mistral-Large-Instruct, Qwen-2.5-72B-Instruct, etc.) it must be declared **before** the run starts; cross-model blending is prohibited.\\n- **Prompt wrapper (identical across all conditions).** Each generated continuation is conditioned on a single string of the form:\\n\\n  ```\\n  <INSTRUCTION_LINE>\\n  Q: <BBH input>\\n  A:\\n  ```\\n\\n  The `<INSTRUCTION_LINE>` is empty for I1, and is the literal instruction text for I2/I3/(I4/I5). **No system role. No few-shot examples. No chat templating tags.** This isolates the instruction-line manipulation as the sole treatment.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Sampling\\n\\n- **Coverage.** All 23 BBH subtasks; stratify by subtask.\\n- **Per-subtask cap.** Up to **250 examples/subtask** from the held-out test split \\u2192 \\u2264 **5,750 prompts/condition**.\\n- **Order.** Concatenate all subtask prompts into a single list per condition; **shuffle with a fixed RNG seed** so all conditions see the same ordering. Record the seed.\\n- **Pilot option (recommended).** 50 examples/subtask (~1,150 prompts/condition, 10\\u00d7 cheaper) to validate extraction and detect qualitatively opposite directions before committing to the full run.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Procedure\\n\\n1. **Vendor** BBH `test_suite` JSONLs into the execution workspace; cross-reference example IDs against the OPRO optimization train/dev list; publish an exclusion list and a final sample-count by subtask.\\n2. **Build** the prompt wrapper for each (condition \\u00d7 subtask \\u00d7 example) triple under a fixed RNG seed; persist generation records (`example_id`, `condition`, `prompt`, `seed`).\\n3. **Generate** continuations under the primary decoding regime (greedy, T=0); persist raw continuations. Caching of prompt encodings is encouraged; **no instruction-conditioned caching of the decode path**.\\n4. **Extract** final answers with the deterministic extractor described in \\u00a78. Run a labeled-sample sanity pass on **50 random extractions** per condition; manually adjudicate any mismatch between extractor output and a human-readable correct answer; report **exact-match accuracy** under this extractor.\\n5. *(Robustness)* repeat step 3 under the T=0.3, n=3, fixed-seed regime and apply **majority-of-3** to the extracted answers; record only the voted answer and the per-example vote distribution.\\n6. **Score** with the metric set in \\u00a71; aggregate per-subtask \\u2192 macro and micro accuracy \\u2192 per-category.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Feasibility (Tiered Budget)\\n\\nToken accounting for the **full** primary pass (4 conditions \\u00d7 5,750 prompts): ~5,750 \\u00d7 (\\u2248600 input + \\u2248150 output) \\u2248 **20.9 M tokens/condition \\u00d7 4 \\u2248 84 M tokens** for greedy; \\u2248 **3\\u00d7 that** for the n=3 robustness pass (\\u2248 250 M tokens). Pilot tier (50/subtask) divides by ~5x.\\n\\n| # | Tier (model \\u00b7 serving) | Cost-tokens / condition | Wall-time per condition | Wall-time for full 4-condition primary | Cost notes | Status |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| T1 | **Sandbox:** LLaMA-3-70B on 24-CPU box, no GPU, no parallel inference framework | \\u2248 20.9 M tokens | many **days/condition** with batch-CPU FP16/BF16 (\\u2248 0.3\\u20130.5 tok/s/sample); up to \\u2248 6 days/condition sustained | \\u2248 **3\\u20134 weeks** for greedy primary alone | OPEX: electricity only | **Infeasible** (cannot stage here at all) |\\n| T2 | LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct hosted (Fireworks / Together / OpenRouter) | \\u2248 20.9 M tokens @ ~$1.2\\u20131.8 /M tok | 25\\u201345 min/condition @ 50 tok/s | \\u2248 2\\u20133 h greedy primary; \\u2248 6\\u20138 h incl. n=3 robustness | \\u2248 $100\\u2013$150 (4 conditions, gpt-budget) | **Default** |\\n| T3 | LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct, **4\\u00d7 A100 / H100, vLLM** | \\u2248 20.9 M tokens | 8\\u201315 min @ ~250 tok/s/sample batch 32 | \\u2248 35\\u201360 min greedy primary | OPEX: cluster time | Excellent fidelity/cost |\\n| T4 | LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct, **single A100/H100, vLLM** | \\u2248 20.9 M tokens | 25\\u201390 min | 1.5\\u20136 h greedy primary | Includes batch_down | Workable; honors 24 h single-GPU constraint |\\n| T5 | 7\\u201313B-class IT (e.g., LLaMA-3-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3), 1\\u00d7 A100 | \\u2248 20.9 M tokens, 7B cost base | 20\\u201340 min | **\\u2260 70B OOM at full size**; recompute on 70B-tier | Lower-**fidelity** (transferability drop) | Acceptable for transferability-first study if compute is tight |\\n| T6 | PaLM 2-L (Vertex AI / MakerSuite) | \\u2248 20.9 M tokens @ ~$1.0\\u20131.4 /M tok | 25\\u201335 min | 1.5\\u20133 h | \\u2248 $90\\u2013$120 | Only meaningful **if** PlayerAccess **and** Vertex is in budget; otherwise skipped |\\n\\n**Recommendation.** **T3** is the safe production choice; **T2** is the best ROI when the executing team prefers managed inference; **T4** is room-and-board under tighter GPU constraints. **T1 (sandbox) is unusable** for this study on the current 24-CPU bubblewrap mount \\u2014 the data point is included for transparency rather than to recommend.\\n\\n**Pilot option.** A 50-per-subtask pilot (T2) is \\u2248 4.2 M tokens total \\u2192 \\u2248 $5\\u2013$8 \\u2192 15\\u201330 minutes wall clock; recommended as a gate before committing the full tier.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Scoring Protocol\\n\\n1. **Answer extraction (deterministic; same across all conditions).**\\n   - Regex tier 1: `(?i)the answer is\\\\s+\\\\(?([-\\\\w/.,]+)\\\\)?` over the last 256 chars.\\n   - Regex tier 2 (multistep arithmetic outputs): trailing token after `#### `, `=`, or final line tokens.\\n   - Regex tier 3 (boolean / multiple-choice): exact-token match against the option set supplied by the subtask.\\n   - Constrained-decoding tier: subtasks with fixed-output grammar (date understanding, web-of-lies, etc.) use a structured parser; reported separately so the comparison to free-form extractions is auditable.\\n2. **Primary metric.** **Macro-accuracy** \\u2014 simple mean of per-subtask accuracies over the 23 subtasks, equal weight per subtask. This is the score Suzgun et al. and Yang et al. report as the BBH composite.\\n3. **Secondary metrics.** Micro-accuracy; per-category mean (algebra, formal logic, natural language, knowledge, multistep arithmetic, common sense, programming); per-subtask accuracy table.\\n4. **Effect sizes.** \\u0394 macro-accuracy expressed in absolute percentage points with bootstrap 95% CIs; per-subtask \\u0394 and per-category \\u0394 reported alongside.\\n5. **Multiple runs.** The **primary** pass is **greedy, single sample**. The **robustness** pass is `T=0.3, n=3`, **majority vote** under a fixed per-(subtask, example-index) seed schedule; this is reported as a check on whether greedy rankings persist under sampling.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Statistical Analysis\\n\\n1. **Per-subtask paired bootstrap** for each (contrast \\u00d7 subtask): sample examples with replacement B=10,000 times from the **intersection** of examples scored by all conditions; compute \\u0394 as macro-accuracy on the resample. Use a global RNG seed.\\n2. **Cross-subtask aggregation** via **Stouffer's z-method** weighted by \\u221an_subtask. **Holm\\u2013Bonferroni** correction within each contrast family across the 23 subtasks. Family-wise \\u03b1 = 0.05.\\n3. **Bootstrap CIs on \\u0394 macro-accuracy** at the **overall (BBH-wide) level**: pool bootstrap-resample per subtask, compute the macro-mean on the resample, report 2.5/97.5 percentile bounds.\\n4. **Sign-test on subtasks**: count subtasks with non-negative \\u0394; compare to a binomial null (p=0.5) at Holm-corrected \\u03b1=0.05. The **\\u2265 16/23 majority-of-subtasks gate (G2)** corresponds to p \\u2248 0.046 (two-sided), which is Holm-consistent against the per-subtask Holm tier for a single primary contrast.\\n5. **Sensitivity analyses (planned, not budget-required).**\\n   - **Template perturbation:** re-run primary pass under a system-role + chat-template wrapper on **25% of the test split** (1,440 prompts/condition) to verify the rank order is not an artifact of the `Q:\\\\nA:` wrapper. (addresses U8).\\n   - **Per-category stratification:** report \\u0394 restricted to the 7 BBH categories to localize OPRO gains.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Why these controls suffice (isolation logic)\\n\\n| Possible confound | How it would bias a naive comparison | How this design isolates it |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **(i) Instruction-text effect** (does *this literal instruction string* matter?) | Reporting only CoT vs. empty would attribute any improvement to \\\"instruction style\\\", not to OPRO specifically. | **I3 vs. I1** (instruction present vs. absent) quantifies the effect of *putting any instruction in the prompt*. **I3 vs. I2** quantifies whether OPRO's specific instruction beats the canonical CoT baseline. Taken together, these isolate the **specific-text contribution** of OPRO. |\\n| **(ii) Generic CoT effect** (is \\\"Take a deep breath\\\" just a CoT-in-structural-form trigger?) | If \\\"Take a deep breath\\\" were a stealth CoT cue, I1/I2 alone would mis-attribute gains. | **I2** is the canonical \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\" CoT baseline, identical low-token-length instruction as the OPRO strings. The **\\u0394\\u2081 contrast** is therefore CoT-style-vs-OPRO; a positive \\u0394\\u2081 cannot be explained by generic \\\"instructions that prompt reasoning\\\" alone. |\\n| **(iii) Instruction-tuning artifact** (does post-training on instruction-tuned data drive the gain?) | Some instruction-tuned chat models apply implicit rewriting to underspecified prompts. | The **identical prompt wrapper** and the **identical model** across all conditions guarantee the instruction-tuned behavior is constant. Any gain is therefore **inside the model, outside the wrapper** \\u2014 i.e., a property of the instruction line itself. The optional **I5 (BBH original per-subtask instruction)** sanity reference further checks whether **any well-tuned instruction** matches I3, isolating whether I3 is better than \\\"the BBH authors' intended phrasing\\\". |\\n| **(iv) Subtask-mix confound** | Different subtasks have very different ceilings (Penguins-in-a-Table vs. Multistep-Arithmetic). | **Macro-accuracy** weights subtasks equally; reported alongside **micro-accuracy** weights by example-count. The opposite-sign disagreement (if any) is itself a finding and is documented. |\\n| **(v) Decoding/non-determinism confound** | Stochastic decoding can move accuracy by 5\\u201310 pp within a model. | Primary pass is **T=0 greedy**. The robustness pass uses a **fixed-seed grid**, so any condition-vs-condition noise difference is upstream of the seed. |\\n| **(vi) Selection / leakage from optimization corpus** | If BBH examples that fed OPRO's meta-search leak into the evaluation set, the comparison is biased. | Held-out **test split only**; explicit ID scrub against the OPRO train/dev IDs; exclusion list published. |\\n\\nNet: the design sets up **two sufficient contrasts** (I3 vs. I1 and I3 vs. I2) plus an **I1-vs-I2 sanity contrast**, with an identical wrapper and identical model. A win for OPRO is therefore a property of **the instruction string under fixed decoding on a single model**, holding wrapper/macro-mix/decoding/leakage constant.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Pre-execution Checklist (G1\\u2013G5)\\n\\nEach item is a yes/no gate; the executing team must initial each before launching the full pass.\\n\\n- **G1. Conditions locked.**\\n  - [ ] I1 = empty, I2 = \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\", I3 = \\\"Take a deep breath\\\".\\n  - [ ] I4 dropped from **primary** contrasts. **(U1 resolved.)**\\n  - [ ] I5 (BBH original per-subtask instruction) = **off**, **on**, or **deferred post-pilot**. Default: **deferred**.\\n\\n- **G2. Model and template locked.** **(U2, U8)**\\n  - [ ] Model = `LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct` (default). Substitute alternatives named and recorded.\\n  - [ ] Prompt wrapper = `<INSTRUCTION_LINE>\\\\nQ: <input>\\\\nA:` with system role = none and no chat template tags.\\n  - [ ] Decoding: T=0.0, top_p=1.0, n=1, max_new_tokens=512 for primary; T=0.3, n=3, fixed-seed grid for robustness.\\n  - [ ] All four conditions (I1, I2, I3, [+I5]) share the wrapper verbatim.\\n\\n- **G3. Scope tier selected.** (Resolution from \\u00a77.)\\n  - [ ] Tier = **T3 (4\\u00d7A100, vLLM)** \\u2190 recommended, or **T2 (hosted)** \\u2190 budget-tight, or other.\\n  - [ ] Pilot pass = **50/subtask** before the full pass (recommended).\\n  - [ ] Wall-time + dollar figures recorded against the architecture choice.\\n\\n- **G4. Spread checks & pre-execution sanity.**\\n  - [ ] BBH `test_suite` JSONLs vendored; example-ID scrub against OPRO train/dev list computed; **exclusion list finalized**.\\n  - [ ] **50-prompt pilot run** succeeded across all conditions: each completion begins with valid English/structured output; idiom hallucination \\u2264 5%; extraction works on \\u226595% of pilot completions per condition.\\n  - [ ] Same RNG seed across conditions; shuffling seed and per-(subtask, example-index) stochastic-seed schedule saved.\\n  - [ ] Extraction tier assignment per subtask documented; controlled-output subtasks identified.\\n\\n- **G5. Analysis lock-down.**\\n  - [ ] Statistical-analysis plan, including bootstrap seed, B=10,000, Stouffer weighting, and Holm\\u2013Bonferroni family structure, **committed before** the full pass; no opportunistic analyses added post-hoc.\\n  - [ ] **Pre-registered superiority criterion** (lower-CI > 0 AND \\u226516/23 subtasks non-negative) signed off and pasted into the final memo template.\\n  - [ ] **Out-of-scope statements** acknowledged: no replication of OPRO's PaLM 2-L headline numbers is claimed; transferability-to-LLaMA-3-70B is the claim-space.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Out of Scope / Not Claimed\\n\\n- No claim is made that experiments have been run; no accuracy numbers appear in this document.\\n- No claim is made about PaLM 2-L replication.\\n- No claim that \\\"Let's work this out in a step by step way...\\\" is an OPRO-discovered leader without independent citation (it's held in exploratory reserve per \\u00a73).\\n- No claim that this design, even if executed, would \\\"settle\\\" OPRO vs. all baselines under every model/decoding regime.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Final Memo Outline (post-execution; designed, not drafted)\\n\\nOnce executed, the team posts a memo in this order: **(1)** Decision rules (\\u00a71 above); **(2)** Conditions and sample counts per subtask post-scrub; **(3)** Primary pass table (I1/I2/I3/I5 \\u2014 macro, micro, per-category); **(4)** Robustness pass table; **(5)** \\u0394 table with bootstrap CIs and Holm-corrected p-values; **(6)** Sign test on subtasks; **(7)** Verdict keyed to the dual-gate criterion; **(8)** Limitations: model portability, wrapper-sensitivity, multiple-comparisons discipline.\\n\\n---\\n*End of design document.*\", \"evidence\": \"Built on the approved first-turn plan. No empirical numbers invented; all feasibility figures are planning estimates against external deployment tiers, with the sandbox tier marked infeasible. U1 was resolved by dropping the second leader from primary scope (honest position the executing team can extend), G1\\u2013G5 fully mapped to a pre-execution checklist, decision rules promoted to the top, transferability framing (not replication) made explicit with LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct as the named default substitute, and an isolation-logic section added that shows how empty + CoT + (optional) original-instruction controls separate generic CoT, raw instruction, and instruction-tuning artifacts under fixed wrapper/decoding/model.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1: 'Take a deep breath' is one of the top OPRO-discovered instructions on BBH per Yang et al., ICLR 2024; cited but not re-derived inside this sandbox.\", \"A2: 'Let's work this out in a step by step way to be sure we have the right answer' is held in exploratory reserve pending independent provenance verification; the design's primary claims are scoped to A1.\", \"A3: BBH 'test_suite' split (23 subtasks) is the evaluation source; OPRO's optimization used a separate train/dev subset which is excluded by ID scrub.\", \"A4: Macro-accuracy (mean of per-subtask accuracies, equal weights) is the BBH-standard primary metric, matching Suzgun et al. and Yang et al.\", \"A5: Primary decoding is greedy (T=0); this mirrors OPRO's headline numbers but is a design choice the executing team must commit to.\", \"A6: The prompt wrapper `<INSTRUCTION_LINE>\\\\nQ: <input>\\\\nA:` is identical across all conditions; only the INSTRUCTION_LINE varies.\", \"A7: The same model (default LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct) serves all conditions; cross-model blending is not allowed.\", \"A8: 'Empty prompt' means no INSTRUCTION_LINE; the rest of the wrapper is shared.\", \"A9: Holm\\u2013Bonferroni correction operates within each contrast family over the 23 subtasks; Stouffer's method aggregates per-subtask bootstrap statistics with \\u221an_subtask weights.\", \"A10: The 250-example/subtask cap is a planning parameter, not a statistic; the pilot pass should confirm it does not flip the sign of any contrast.\", \"A11: The token budget per example (\\u2248600 input + \\u2248150 output) is a planning figure drawn from typical BBH prompt/output sizes as reported in the literature, not from local measurements.\", \"A12: The 16/23 majority-of-subtasks threshold aligns with a Holm-consistent binomial test against p=0.5 within the per-subtask Holm family for the primary contrast.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1: Provenance of the second instruction is unverified in this sandbox; resolved by dropping it from the primary contrasts and holding it in exploratory reserve.\", \"U2: Choice of substitute model affects transferability claims; substituting away from LLaMA-3-70B-Instruct induces a model-portability caveat.\", \"U3: The sandbox cannot execute this experiment (no network, no GPU, no ML deps); no result is claimed; feasibility refers to external execution tiers (T2\\u2013T6).\", \"U4: The scrub of OPRO train/dev IDs from the BBH evaluation set requires a copy of the OPRO optimization corpus at execution time; without it, the design's guarantee of leakage-freeness is partial.\", \"U5: Greedy decoding must be uniformly available across all backend alternatives; a backend that only supports sampling introduces a confounding input the design does not control for.\", \"U6: Local data-embedding rules at the execution site may require re-vendoring BBH separately from this workspace.\", \"U7: Multiple-comparisons discipline (per-subtask Holm) must be pre-registered; without it, the experiment is at higher false-discovery risk.\", \"U8: Prompt-template detail sensitivity is mitigated by the planned 25% template-perturbation sensitivity check on the test split.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-5b022b2c-e19a-4a4c-9b24-4551f3aae6ec", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
